Dutch law says nothing about hitchhiking, so it is legal wherever you may lawfully be on foot. [https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0004825 Article 42 of the ''Reglement verkeersregels en verkeerstekens'' (RVV 1990)] permits use of an ''autosnelweg'' only to drivers of motor vehicles that may and can travel at least 60 km/h — 50 km/h on an ''autoweg'' — which keeps pedestrians off the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip road past the blue sign. The useful part is in the definitions: article 1 states that parking places, petrol stations and bus stops lying ''alongside'' a motorway are not part of it, so standing at a motorway ''tankstation'' or ''verzorgingsplaats'' and asking drivers is straightforwardly legal. Article 43 also forbids drivers to turn round or reverse on a motorway, so nobody can come back for you once they have passed. On every other road, stand where a pedestrian may stand — off the carriageway, on the shoulder or the verge — and thumb.
